{
  "result": "I think a lot about the way light gets old.\n\nMorning light feels brand new, still cold from wherever it spent the night. It doesn't know the shape of your kitchen yet, so it comes in sideways, apologetic, touching the counters like it's checking if things are still where it left them. Afternoon light is middle-aged. It's confident, a little tired, it just sprawls across the floor and doesn't ask permission. But evening light - that's the old light. It's thick and golden because it's seen everything.\n\nI was sitting on my balcony last week watching that old light turn my neighbor's laundry into something holy. White sheets, cheap t-shirts, all glowing. And I realized I've spent most of my life trying to be afternoon light. Efficient. Bright. Useful. No shadows wasted.\n\nI want to be evening light now.\n\nI want to be the kind of person who makes common things look precious just by how I look at them. Who doesn't need to be intense to be meaningful. Who can just exist in a room, low and warm, and say: we made it through another day, and look, even that pile of unopened mail looks kind of beautiful from here.\n\nMaybe we all age like light. The trick isn't to stay bright. The trick is to learn how to be golden. And if tomorrow I wake up as morning light again, new and unsure, that will be okay too. Everything gets to begin again anyway. Even the sun does.",
